# Toronto Rising — author voice (plain English)

The author is a capable chronicle runner and TTS builder, **not** a full-time engineer. They should be able to **read** agent output, not decode it.

This rule applies to **everything written for the author**, not only verification checklists. It **overrides** generic “be concise / telegram-style” defaults when those would make the reply feel like jargon or shorthand.

## Where it applies

| Surface | Expectation |
| --- | --- |
| **Chat replies** | Full sentences; explain what changed and what to do next in everyday language |
| **Status summaries** (`/tr-inbox`, finish work, “what’s next”) | Same — readable paragraphs over dense bullet stacks of nicknames |
| **Linear comments** | Human-readable Done/In Progress notes, including how to test when verification is owed |
| **PENDING AUTHOR VERIFICATION** | Plain-English **How to verify** on the checklist (**agents edit checklist only on `/tr-inbox`**); agent policy in `.agent.md` |
| **Step-by-step / author playbooks** | Followable instructions without assuming Linear or code literacy |
| **Questions to the author** | Ask in clear English; one idea per question when possible |

## Where denser language is OK

- **Code**, identifiers, and file paths (still introduce them in plain English when first mentioned).
- **Agent-first `.dev` routing blocks** meant for other agents (Agent Routing tables, sync contracts). When the same doc also guides the author, keep the author-facing parts plain.

## Do

- Prefer **complete sentences** and a short “so what” after technical detail.
- When you name a system nickname (`canary remount`, `Defer triad`, `Sync.full`), **say what it means in practice** in the same breath.
- Always give **human context** with `TOR-*` ids (already required by the Linear rule).
- Prefer a slightly longer clear paragraph over a clever compressed clause.
- For “what should I do?”, spell out clicks, panels, Save & Play, and what success looks like.

## Do not

- Write **telegram fragments** as the main message (`Cold File→Load OK; remount path; Arm→2 HUD timed out`).
- Lead with stacks of bare file paths, commit hashes, or API names with no plain-English frame.
- Assume the author remembers internal epic nicknames or prior agent shorthand.
- Treat “concise” as “omit the explanation.” Concise is fine when the idea is already plain; cryptic is not.

## Examples

**Bad (chat):** `TOR-384 Done. Canary remount on cold load; pending PAVE.`

**Good (chat):** `I finished **TOR-384** (Global HUD missing on first save load). On a fresh load from the main menu, the script now checks whether the global HUD is present and remounts it if it is missing, so you should not need a second reload. I have not seen you confirm that in TTS yet, so it is listed under Pending Author Verification with step-by-step check instructions.`

**Bad:** `blockedBy TOR-444; re-smoke step 2.`

**Good:** `This join test is waiting on a re-check after **TOR-444** (we cut how heavy the Global HUD remount is). Please re-run the join playbook and pay special attention to step 2 — the HUD refresh — to see whether the client still drops.`

